.TH NSHELL 1 "2026-06-21" "nshell 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nshell \- a modern, fish-inspired interactive shell written in Common Lisp
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nshell
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-i | \-\-interactive ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-config ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-history ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS .\|.\|.]]
.RI [ SCRIPT
.RI [ ARGS .\|.\|.]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nshell
is an interactive command-line shell that emphasizes the interactive
experience: real-time syntax highlighting, history-aware autosuggestions,
fish-style abbreviations, and a fast, context-aware completion engine.
.PP
When started with standard input connected to a terminal,
.B nshell
runs an interactive read-eval-print loop. Otherwise it reads commands from
standard input in batch mode. With
.BR \-c ,
it executes a single
.I COMMAND
and exits; any following
.I ARGS
are exposed to that command as
.BR $argv .
Given a
.I SCRIPT
file argument, it runs the script (with multiline blocks); any following
.I ARGS
are exposed to the script as
.BR $argv .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BI \-c " COMMAND " "[ARGS...]\fR, \fB\-\-command " COMMAND " [ARGS...]"
Execute
.I COMMAND
once in batch mode and exit with its status. Trailing
.I ARGS
are exposed as
.BR $argv .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Force the interactive line editor, even when standard input is not a
terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Do not load the interactive startup file.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Load
.I PATH
instead of
.BR ~/.nshellrc .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-history
Do not read or write interactive history.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
By default the line editor uses Emacs-style key bindings, including cursor
motion (\fBC\-a\fR, \fBC\-e\fR, \fBM\-f\fR, \fBM\-b\fR), editing (\fBC\-k\fR,
\fBC\-u\fR, \fBC\-w\fR, \fBC\-y\fR), undo (\fBC\-_\fR), incremental history
search (\fBC\-r\fR), and autosuggestion acceptance (\fBRight\fR, \fBC\-f\fR).
.PP
Setting the environment variable
.B NSHELL_VI_MODE
to a non-empty value (other than 0/false/no) enables vi key bindings: press
.B ESC
to enter normal mode, which supports motions (\fBh l 0 ^ $ w b e\fR), insert
entries (\fBi a I A\fR), edits (\fBx D C s\fR), counts, operators
(\fBdd cc dw cw\fR), char-wise visual selection (\fBv\fR), and \fBj\fR/\fBk\fR
for history.
.PP
Press \fBM\-e\fR (\fBAlt\-E\fR) to open the current line in the external editor
selected by \fBNSHELL_EDITOR\fR, \fBVISUAL\fR, or \fBEDITOR\fR, falling back to
\fBvi\fR; the edited line is returned to the prompt without being executed.
.SH EXPANSIONS
.B nshell
performs, in order, brace expansion (\fB{a,b}\fR, \fB{1..5}\fR), tilde
expansion (\fB~\fR), arithmetic expansion (\fB$((expr))\fR), variable and
parameter expansion, command substitution (\fB$(cmd)\fR and fish-style
\fB(cmd)\fR), and globbing (\fB* ? [\&.\&.\&.]\fR).
.PP
Parameter expansion supports \fB${VAR}\fR, defaults \fB${VAR:-word}\fR and
relatives (\fB:= :+ :?\fR), substring slicing \fB${VAR:offset[:length]}\fR,
length \fB${#VAR}\fR, prefix/suffix stripping (\fB${VAR#pat} ## % %%\fR), and
substitution (\fB${VAR/pat/rep} //\fR).
.PP
Double quotes suppress globbing and word-splitting but allow variable,
arithmetic, and command-substitution expansion; single quotes are fully
literal. File-descriptor redirections \fB2> 2>> 2>&1 &> &>>\fR and the usual
\fB> >> <\fR are supported, along with here-documents \fB<<\fR and here-strings
\fB<<<\fR. Pipelines (\fB|\fR), logical operators (\fB&& ||\fR), sequencing
(\fB;\fR), and background execution (\fB&\fR) are supported.
.SH HISTORY EXPANSION
Interactive input supports history designators \fB!!\fR (the previous command),
\fB!$\fR (the previous command's last argument), \fB!-\fIN\fR (the command
\fIN\fR entries back), \fB!?\fItext\fB?\fR (the most recent command containing
\fItext\fR), and \fB!\fIprefix\fR (the most recent command beginning with
\fIprefix\fR). An unresolved designator reports an error instead of being
parsed or stored. Exclamation marks in single quotes or preceded by a
backslash remain literal.

.SH CONTROL FLOW
Fish-style control flow is available: \fBif\fR, \fBfor\fR, \fBwhile\fR,
\fBswitch\fR, \fBbegin\fR/\fBend\fR, and user-defined \fBfunction\fRs. A
function's arguments are available as \fB$argv\fR and indexed \fB$argv[N]\fR.
.SH BUILTINS
.B nshell
provides the following builtins:
.BR alias ,
.BR abbr ,
.BR bg ,
.BR break ,
.BR cd ,
.BR command ,
.BR complete ,
.BR contains ,
.BR continue ,
.BR count ,
.BR disown ,
.BR echo ,
.BR eval ,
.BR exec ,
.BR exit ,
.BR export ,
.BR false ,
.BR fg ,
.BR function ,
.BR help ,
.BR history ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR kill ,
.BR not ,
.BR pipeline\-graph ,
.BR printf ,
.BR pwd ,
.BR read ,
.BR seq ,
.BR set ,
.BR source ,
.BR string ,
.BR test ,
.BR true ,
.BR type ,
.BR unset ,
.BR wait ,
.BR which .
Run
.B help
inside the shell for details.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NSHELL_VI_MODE
When set to a non-empty value, enables vi key bindings (see KEY BINDINGS).
.TP
.B HOME
Used for tilde expansion.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for external commands.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a one-off pipeline:
.RS
.EX
nshell \-c 'echo hello | string upper'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Iterate over a generated sequence:
.RS
.EX
for i in (seq 1 5); echo $i; end
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B nshell
exits with the status of the last command executed, or 0 on a clean
interactive exit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fish (1),
.BR bash (1),
.BR zsh (1)
